
Lyntin has many of the same features as Tintin and the commands
are based on Tintin.  However, there are a series of Tintin
commands we do not currently support for various reasons:

   * some because Lyntin is written in Python and exposes the user
     to the Python interpreter making a series of the commands kind
     of silly
   * a few because Lyntin has better ways to do things
   * a paltry few because we haven't gotten around to it

If you miss some Tintin things, it's pretty easy to implement your
own commands in Lyntin.  See the ``HACKING`` document for more
information.

There are two user input evaluation modes in Lyntin: Lyntin mode and
Tintin mode.  You can switch modes using the command-line argument::

   --evalmode tintin
   or
   --evalmode lyntin

or ingame with::

   #config evalmode lyntin
   or
   #config evalmode tintin

The evalmodes differ in how variables and placement variables are
expanded as well as the time at which they're expanded.  Tintin mode
emulates how Tintin works.  Lyntin mode is born from our own preferred
user input evaluation.  There are probably good and bad points of 
each which is beyond the scope of this document.

Note: Lyntin defaults to lyntin mode--so if you're a Tintin user and 
want to user your Tintin command files, you should make sure to start 
Lyntin up in tintin mode.

